// Concepts — the ideas a WAD author or populating agent must hold.

concept what_is_a_wad {
  name    = "What a WAD is"
  summary = "A living architecture document: one typed WCL data model of a system, rendered into a twelve-chapter book."
  related = [twelve_views, c4_drilldown, consumption_modes, wad_layout]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p "A WAD (Wil's Architecture Document) is a folder — `wad.wcl` + `schema/` + `data/` + `wdoc/` — whose **data model is the document**. Every architecture fact is a typed block instance (a `system`, a `relation`, an `adr`); every diagram, table and page in the rendered book derives from those blocks. Nothing is drawn by hand."
    p "The shape is arc42-inspired (a fixed set of views a reviewer can navigate blind) over a C4 drill-down (system → container → component → code). It serves three jobs: **track** an existing system honestly, **design** a new one in enough detail to review, and **drive change** — a reviewed WAD revision becomes the baseline that `wcl diff` turns edits into specs against."
    p "Create one with `wcl init wad`; validate with `wcl check wad.wcl`; the whole model either checks or it doesn't — there is no partially-valid WAD."
  }
}

concept twelve_views {
  name    = "The twelve views"
  summary = "The fixed chapter set of every WAD book, and which block families feed each."
  related = [what_is_a_wad, wad_layout]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p "Every WAD renders the same twelve chapters, so a reader always knows where a fact lives. When filing data, pick the view first:"
    table {
      header = ["#", "View", "Answers", "Block families"]
      rows = [
        ["1", "Overview", "What is this, who cares, what was decided?", "`wad`, `stakeholder`, `raci_entry`, `adr`"],
        ["2", "System context", "How does the estate sit in the world?", "`relation` (the diagram derives)"],
        ["3", "External systems", "What third parties does it *integrate with* to do its job? (Platforms it merely runs/ships on are view 5.)", "`external_system` (+ contacts, endpoints, api_refs, security notes)"],
        ["4", "Systems", "What are we made of? (C4 drill-down)", "`system`, `container`, `component`, `cli_command`, `code_item`, `screen`"],
        ["5", "Infrastructure", "Where does it run, per environment?", "`environment`, `infra_node`, `deploy_target`"],
        ["6", "Build & deploy", "How does a change become a running system?", "`repository`, `pipeline` (+ stages), `release`"],
        ["7", "Documentation", "What does the project publish to be read, and who serves it?", "`doc_item`"],
        ["8", "User personas", "Who *uses* it, how are they gated, what do they typically do?", "`persona` (+ access grants), `use_case`"],
        ["9", "System admin", "How is it operated?", "`sop` (+ steps and flow)"],
        ["10", "Standards & rules", "What is the code/business held to?", "`standard` (+ rules)"],
        ["11", "Domain", "What is the model made of?", "`domain_object` (+ fields, relations)"],
        ["12", "Specs", "What changes are in flight?", "`spec` (+ changes)"],
      ]
    }
    p "Freeform topics that fit no family become `article` blocks, filed under a view with `section = :overview` (etc.)."
    p "**Documentation is an output, not a system.** A docs site, book, or skill folder is never a `system`/`container` in view 4 — it files as a `doc_item` in the documentation view, recording where it's authored, what builds it, where it's hosted, and which personas it serves."
  }
}

concept c4_drilldown {
  name    = "The C4 drill-down"
  summary = "System → container → component → code item: the four nesting levels and what belongs at each."
  related = [twelve_views, relations_model]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p "View 4 decomposes the estate in four strict levels:"
    list {
      li "**System** — one box when you explain the estate to an outsider. Owns containers."
      li "**Container** — the independently runnable/deployable unit: a service, a database, a web app, a CLI, a message queue, a static site. The test: *could this run or ship on its own?* (All WAD blocks live in the `wcl.wad` namespace, so the kind is plain `container` — it never collides with wdoc's diagram shape of the same name.)"
      li "**Component** — a cohesive part inside a container: a module, a handler family, a store. No deployment identity of its own."
      li "**Code item** — a diagram of code-level structure: a module graph, a DB schema, a class diagram, or an API surface (`kind` selects the renderer). Code items are **generated by extractor scripts** and document the **public interface only** — never hand-drawn mirrors of internal structure."
    }
    p "The drill-down stops at interfaces: internals below the public surface change too often to document and are better read from the code itself."
  }
}

concept data_vs_presentation {
  name    = "Data vs presentation"
  summary = "Architecture facts live in data/; the book's look lives in wdoc/ templates. Fix each in its own place."
  related = [what_is_a_wad, generated_vs_hand]
  body {
    p "A WAD keeps content and rendering strictly apart: `data/` holds block instances, `wdoc/book/` holds projections over the gathered lists. Consequences: a wrong fact is fixed in data (never by editing a page); a layout problem is fixed in a template (never by contorting data); and `wcl check wad.wcl` validates the model regardless of how it renders."
    p "The templates derive everything — context and container diagrams from `relation` roll-ups, the ER diagram from `domain_object` fields, pipelines from stage DAGs — so data stays the single source of truth."
  }
}

concept generated_vs_hand {
  name    = "Generated vs hand-authored data"
  summary = "Two ownership zones under data/: extractor-owned generated/ files (overwritten wholesale) and human-owned everything else."
  related = [extractor_anatomy, living_wad]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p "Files under `data/generated/` (and any `data/<view>/generated/`) belong to extractor scripts: each script fully overwrites its one output file on every run, and the file's banner names its owner. **Never hand-edit a generated file** — the next run silently reverts you; fix the extractor instead."
    p "Hand-authored data may *reference* generated ids (a hand-written relation can point at a script-emitted container) but never reuses them. Generated files are **committed**, not gitignored: the diff→spec workflow materialises old git revisions, and uncommitted generated data would vanish from the old side of every diff."
  }
}

concept spec_lifecycle {
  name    = "Specs and the change workflow"
  summary = "A spec is implementation detail for an AI agent to build part of the system; WAD edits are decomposed into specs by an LLM."
  related = [living_wad, wcl_diff_wad, spec_blocks]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p "A **spec** is not a diff — it is the worked-out detail an AI coding agent needs to implement *part of the system*: scope, instructions, acceptance criteria. The WAD stays the source of truth for what the system *is*; specs are the work packages that get it built or changed."
    p "The change workflow: edit the WAD data to describe the target state and get it reviewed. Then an LLM (with a human shaping the cut — decomposition is hard to get right) reads the delta between the reviewed baseline and the new state — `git diff`, `wcl diff <rev>:wad.wcl wad.wcl`, or a `wcl wad spec` skeleton, which pins the baseline sha and the exact entity/field change list — and writes one or more specs sized for the implementing model."
    p "How much decomposition depends on the implementer: a strong model can often implement directly from the WAD and the diff; smaller models need the change broken into narrow, explicit specs. Status tracks the work honestly: `:planning → :in_progress → :complete` (or `:abandoned`); the merged revision becomes the next baseline."
  }
}

concept consumption_modes {
  name    = "Who consumes what"
  summary = "Humans review the rendered book; AI agents work from the WAD data and the specs decomposed from it."
  related = [what_is_a_wad, spec_lifecycle]
  body {
    p "The book is **for humans**: `wcl editor wdoc/book/main.wcl` previews the live twelve-chapter site with click-to-comment review (comments land in a `comments.wcl` sidecar; the review handshake blocks an agent until the reviewer sends them)."
    p "AI agents don't get a rendered book — they work from the **WAD itself** (the raw `data/*.wcl` files are typed and legible) and, for implementation work, from the **specs** decomposed out of it. A strong model can implement straight from the WAD; weaker coding models are handed generated specs scoped to what they can hold. See the spec workflow."
  }
}

concept populate_strategies {
  name    = "Interview vs scan"
  summary = "The two population strategies — interview the user (new design) or scan a codebase (existing system) — and when to use each."
  related = [designing_new_system, documenting_existing_system, interview_question_bank, scan_checklist]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p "Two processes converge on the same block model:"
    list {
      li "**Greenfield** (nothing built yet) → run the *design interview*: ask the user one view at a time, write blocks as answers land. The question bank is data — see the interview checklist."
      li "**Existing system** → run the *codebase scan* first: cheap, high-signal sources (repo layout, CI config, entry points, config files) fill most views mechanically; then a targeted mini-interview covers what code can't say (contacts, approvals, off-repo infrastructure)."
    }
    p "Hybrid is normal: scan what exists, interview the rest. Either way, never invent — a gap is recorded as an open question, not filled with a guess."
  }
}

concept relations_model {
  name    = "Relations wire the diagrams"
  summary = "One `relation` block family drives every generated C4 diagram, via roll-up."
  related = [c4_drilldown, context_blocks]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p "A `relation` is a directed edge between two node ids — any mix of systems, containers, components, externals, and personas, which share one id space. Author each relation once, at the **lowest meaningful level** (container↔container, container↔external, persona↔container)."
    p "Diagrams above that level *derive*: the system-context diagram rolls both endpoints up to their owning systems, drops self-edges, and merges parallel edges (labels joined); each system's container diagram rolls to container level and keeps edges touching that system. Re-drawing an edge at a higher level is always wrong — it double-counts on roll-up."
    p "`kind` (a closed vocabulary: `:sync_api`, `:async_msg`, `:reads`, `:publishes`, …) supplies the default edge label; `label`, `protocol` and `data` refine it."
  }
}

concept living_wad {
  name    = "A WAD stays current"
  summary = "The document is maintained continuously — drift between WAD and reality is the failure mode the format exists to prevent."
  related = [generated_vs_hand, spec_lifecycle, keeping_wad_current, lifecycle]
  body {
    p "A WAD written once and abandoned is worse than no WAD — it answers questions wrongly with confidence. The format fights drift three ways: extractors re-derive machine-readable facts from the live system on every run; `wcl diff` against the last reviewed revision makes any drift visible as a change list; and decisions are captured as `adr` blocks at the moment they're made, not reconstructed later."
    p "The habits: after a meaningful change lands, re-run the extractors, update the hand-authored blocks the change invalidates, and record new decisions. Periodically, have an LLM read the **git commit history since the last sweep** and flag what the WAD hasn't caught up with — commits are the change log the document must answer to. The keep-current process turns all of this into a runbook — and build mode's completion step invokes it, so pipeline-driven changes never rely on memory."
  }
}

concept lifecycle {
  name    = "The lifecycle: how the four modes hand off"
  summary = "plan and issue produce plans, build executes them, doc records the result — and every seam is an explicit step, not a convention."
  related = [plan_wad, issue_mode, living_wad, documenting_existing_system, evolving_and_spec, keeping_wad_current, proc_orchestrator_loop]
  body {
    p "**New software:** plan mode scaffolds the plan *and* a WAD of the future system (`just wad-init`), keeps that WAD extracted from the plan as it grows, and hands the rendered briefs to build mode. Build mode re-extracts the WAD as spec statuses move, and at completion **graduates** it: the plan's WAD moves into the built repo (conventionally `.wad/`), sheds `extract_plan.py`, gains the code extractors, and the doc-mode scan backfills what the plan never knew. From then on the repo has a living WAD."
    p "**Changing software:** two entry points, one convergence. *Issue-first* (a bug or feature): issue mode's recon starts by reading the living WAD, the plan executes through build mode, and completion updates the WAD (the keeping-current sweep; `wcl wad spec --from <pre-build-rev>` when the change turned out architectural). *WAD-first* (an architecture change): edit the WAD's target state, derive the delta with `wcl wad spec`, and either implement directly (small change, strong model) or seed an issue-mode plan from the delta — the evolving-and-spec runbook's bridge. Either way the merged revision becomes the WAD's next reviewed baseline."
    p "**Choosing the entry point:** if the work is best described as *behaviour* (\"X is broken\", \"add Y\"), start issue-first — recon will tell you whether the architecture moves. If it is best described as *structure* (\"split the monolith's auth into a service\", \"swap the queue\"), start WAD-first — editing the target state first makes the review happen at the architecture level, where it is cheapest. Capability specs (behaviour, EARS statements under plans/capabilities/) and the WAD (structure) are updated together at build completion; they are the two halves of the repo's memory."
  }
}
